  "verbalization": "Marimastat exhibits a strong binding interaction with multiple matrix metalloproteinase (MMP) isoforms, including Mmp23b, Mmp26, and Mmp27, all of which are part of the MMP family. This binding is supported by a high final score of 0.6336 across three distinct paths, indicating a consistent and significant relationship. The presence of these MMP isoforms, which are involved in the degradation of extracellular matrix components, suggests that Marimastat may exert its effects through a mechanism that targets the MMP pathway. The shared role of these MMPs in the same biological process, as indicated by their participation in the MMP family, implies that Marimastat may inhibit MMP activity in a general manner, potentially affecting tissue remodeling and inflammation. The integrated evidence supports a mechanism where Marimastat binds to MMPs, thereby modulating their activity, which could have therapeutic implications in conditions involving excessive MMP activity.",
